peach-picker I discovered.

266
00:17:37,890 --> 00:17:40,068
I made him into
T.J. Cassidy. Ah.

267
00:17:40,092 --> 00:17:41,858
Whole thing was my idea.

268
00:17:41,894 --> 00:17:44,895
My client said that he saw you

269
00:17:44,930 --> 00:17:48,031
having a big fight with
him the day of the murder.

270
00:17:48,067 --> 00:17:49,867
Oh, yeah. Oh, yeah.

271
00:17:49,902 --> 00:17:52,469
I mean, his expense
account was astronomical.

272
00:17:52,504 --> 00:17:55,139
Hell, I was about
to fire him anyway.

273
00:17:55,174 --> 00:17:57,141
I'll just go discover
somebody else.

274
00:17:57,176 --> 00:17:59,477
Maybe I'll do the TV
commercials myself, huh?

275
00:17:59,512 --> 00:18:01,323
Oh, yeah.

276
00:18:01,347 --> 00:18:03,314
You know, an interesting thing,

277
00:18:03,349 --> 00:18:07,551
after the murder, the
key was still in the ignition

278
00:18:07,586 --> 00:18:10,387
of Mr. Cassidy's car and
the engine was still running

279
00:18:10,423 --> 00:18:12,657
when my client and
the police arrived.

280
00:18:12,692 --> 00:18:13,758
Yeah?

281
00:18:13,793 --> 00:18:16,193
And according to
the police report,

282
00:18:16,229 --> 00:18:18,173
Mr. Cassidy had
another key in his pocket.

283
00:18:18,197 --> 00:18:20,075
How many keys
were there to that car?

284
00:18:20,099 --> 00:18:22,065
I don't know.

285
00:18:22,101 --> 00:18:23,367
A couple of sets maybe.

286
00:18:23,402 --> 00:18:25,703
Who else had access to them?

287
00:18:25,738 --> 00:18:27,638
Jessie Martin.

288
00:18:27,674 --> 00:18:29,373
A lady?

289
00:18:29,408 --> 00:18:31,175
Yeah, my head salesperson.

290
00:18:31,211 --> 00:18:34,345
Were she and T.J. close?

291
00:18:34,380 --> 00:18:36,480
Yeah, very.

292
00:18:36,516 --> 00:18:38,582
Until she caught
T.J. closing a deal

293
00:18:38,617 --> 00:18:40,217
in the back seat of a car.

294
00:18:41,354 --> 00:18:43,654
That could be motive I guess.

295
00:18:43,690 --> 00:18:46,991
But since you were
going to fire him anyway,

296
00:18:47,026 --> 00:18:49,126
and maybe do the
commercials yourself

297
00:18:49,161 --> 00:18:50,995
I guess you didn't
have a motive.

298
00:18:51,030 --> 00:18:52,830
Yep.

299
00:18:52,866 --> 00:18:54,910
Well, I mean why kill a fella

300
00:18:54,934 --> 00:18:56,779
when all you have
to do is fire him?

301
00:18:56,803 --> 00:18:58,335
That makes a lot of sense.

302
00:18:58,371 --> 00:19:00,971
Makes a lot of
sense... So, uh, uh,

303
00:19:01,006 --> 00:19:03,140
nobody else had
access to those keys?

304
00:19:03,175 --> 00:19:04,609
No.

305
00:19:04,644 --> 00:19:06,243
Except you, of course?

306
00:19:06,278 --> 00:19:08,012
Well, sure, I had access.

307
00:19:08,047 --> 00:19:09,580
I mean, I do own the store.

308
00:19:09,616 --> 00:19:12,416
Oh, well, yeah...
Your car's out there,

309
00:19:12,452 --> 00:19:14,296
right next to that
two-toned gray truck.

310
00:19:14,320 --> 00:19:15,652
Oh, okay.

311
00:19:15,688 --> 00:19:17,288
Yeah, I see what you mean.

312
00:19:17,323 --> 00:19:18,689
Why-why kill a fella

313
00:19:18,724 --> 00:19:20,636
when all you
need do is fire him.

314
00:19:31,171 --> 00:19:32,036
Hi.

315
00:19:32,071 --> 00:19:33,504
Les!

316
00:19:33,540 --> 00:19:34,772
How we doing with him?

317
00:19:34,807 --> 00:19:36,574
Now, Les... Any luck?

318
00:19:36,609 --> 00:19:38,142
I don't want to
jump to conclusions,

319
00:19:38,178 --> 00:19:40,210
but I'm sure he's our man.

320
00:19:40,246 --> 00:19:42,546
And I think that fight was
about something more

321
00:19:42,581 --> 00:19:43,881
than his expense account.

322
00:19:43,916 --> 00:19:46,083
And he had opportunity.

323
00:19:46,119 --> 00:19:47,651
It's classic.

324
00:19:47,686 --> 00:19:53,056
Les, I want you to listen to me.

325
00:19:53,092 --> 00:19:56,627
You're not doing
yourself or your case

326
00:19:56,662 --> 00:19:58,729
any good by being here.

327
00:19:58,764 --> 00:20:00,831
You know that.

328
00:20:00,867 --> 00:20:03,934
And you know what they say...

329
00:20:03,970 --> 00:20:06,871
The killer always returns
to the scene of the crime.

330
00:20:13,546 --> 00:20:17,548
So you knew Mr. Cassidy
was working late last night?

331
00:20:17,583 --> 00:20:20,551
Well, T.J. was a
real workaholic.

332
00:20:20,586 --> 00:20:24,588
He worked on commercials,
customers... female of course.

333
00:20:24,623 --> 00:20:26,257
Right.

334
00:20:26,292 --> 00:20:28,837
But when I left last
night T.J. wasn't alone.

335
00:20:28,861 --> 00:20:30,761
George Brenner was here.

336
00:20:30,796 --> 00:20:33,330
Interesting.

337
00:20:33,366 --> 00:20:36,133
Uh, Ms. Martin, could
you tell me where you were

338
00:20:36,169 --> 00:20:37,435
at the time of the murder.

339
00:20:37,470 --> 00:20:40,871
Me? Well, I was at home
watching the ice skating special.

340
00:20:40,906 --> 00:20:42,826
You know, all the
Olympic winners?

341
00:20:44,310 --> 00:20:46,376
Well, thank you for your time.

342
00:21:02,194 --> 00:21:03,326
Ben.

343
00:21:03,362 --> 00:21:05,095
Yeah?

344
00:21:05,130 --> 00:21:07,631
Jessie Martin just lied
to me about her alibi

345
00:21:07,666 --> 00:21:08,777
for last night. Yeah?

346
00:21:08,801 --> 00:21:10,066
Yeah.

347
00:21:10,102 --> 00:21:12,414
She said she was home
watching an ice skating special.

348
00:21:12,438 --> 00:21:13,404
It wasn't on.

349
00:21:13,439 --> 00:21:14,672
No?

350
00:21:14,707 --> 00:21:15,707
No.

351
00:21:15,741 --> 00:21:17,552
I was going to watch
the same show,

352
00:21:17,576 --> 00:21:19,743
but the basketball
game went into overtime

353
00:21:19,778 --> 00:21:20,845
and it never aired.

354
00:21:20,880 --> 00:21:23,613
Oh.

355
00:21:23,649 --> 00:21:25,115
Find Tyler.

356
00:21:44,570 --> 00:21:46,050
Oh.

357
00:22:59,912 --> 00:23:01,623
Made the arrangements
for tomorrow night.

358
00:23:01,647 --> 00:23:03,313
No problem.

359
00:23:32,344 --> 00:23:33,977
So... that's it.

360
00:23:34,012 --> 00:23:35,946
She went to the bar.

361
00:23:35,981 --> 00:23:37,948
Then, she drove to the motel.

362
00:23:37,983 --> 00:23:39,783
And then, bam!

363
00:23:39,819 --> 00:23:42,519
Ouch. Ooh, ooh, sorry.

364
00:23:42,555 --> 00:23:45,121
Well... the
swelling's gone down.

365
00:23:45,157 --> 00:23:46,200
I'll get more ice.

366
00:23:46,224 --> 00:23:47,724
No, no, no, thanks.

367
00:23:47,760 --> 00:23:49,460
I... I'm all right.

368
00:23:49,495 --> 00:23:51,495
No, I'm not all right.

369
00:23:51,530 --> 00:23:54,164
I woke up face-down in the dirt.

370
00:23:54,199 --> 00:23:55,466
I hate that.

371
00:23:55,501 --> 00:23:56,645
You know, I'm confused.

372
00:23:56,669 --> 00:23:58,346
Aren't they going
to a lot of trouble

373
00:23:58,370 --> 00:23:59,837
to cover up an affair?

374
00:24:01,474 --> 00:24:03,574
Maybe this isn't an affair.

375
00:24:06,378 --> 00:24:07,677
What else could it be?

376
00:24:08,814 --> 00:24:11,014
I don't know, but she
said something big

377
00:24:11,050 --> 00:24:12,916
was going to happen
tomorrow night.

378
00:24:12,952 --> 00:24:15,351
And it didn't sound
like romance to me.

379
00:24:31,603 --> 00:24:33,904
Mr. Edwards?

380
00:24:36,475 --> 00:24:38,475
Mr. Ed...

381
00:24:45,084 --> 00:24:46,750
Ben?

382
00:24:48,454 --> 00:24:50,654
Les, what are you doing here?

383
00:24:50,690 --> 00:24:52,734
What if somebody would
come in here besides me?

384
00:24:52,758 --> 00:24:54,936
Do you realize the
danger, the trouble?

385
00:24:54,960 --> 00:24:56,227
Okay, okay, I'm sorry.

386
00:24:56,262 --> 00:24:57,972
Sorry don't feed the bulldog.

387
00:24:57,996 --> 00:25:00,964
What do I have to do to
convince you this is not a game?

388
00:25:00,999 --> 00:25:02,877
You're going on
trial for murder.

389
00:25:02,901 --> 00:25:05,002
I know... that's why I'm here.

390
00:25:05,037 --> 00:25:07,304
I was wrong about
George Brenner.

391
00:25:07,339 --> 00:25:08,939
Honest Earl did it.

392
00:25:08,974 --> 00:25:11,108
Les... Look, you
told me that before

393
00:25:11,143 --> 00:25:12,954
you could accuse a man,
you had to have proof.

394
00:25:12,978 --> 00:25:14,122
Yes. All right, here it is.

395
00:25:14,146 --> 00:25:16,212
Look at this.

396
00:25:16,248 --> 00:25:17,481
Honest Earl did it.

397
00:25:17,516 --> 00:25:18,627
Where did you get this?

398
00:25:18,651 --> 00:25:19,950
Right here.

399
00:25:19,985 --> 00:25:21,185
It's a contract.

400
00:25:23,355 --> 00:25:25,389
Honest Earl...

401
00:25:25,424 --> 00:25:29,193
was trying to lure T.J.
away from Brenner.

402
00:25:29,228 --> 00:25:30,360
How about that.

403
00:25:30,396 --> 00:25:31,662
Yeah.

404
00:25:31,697 --> 00:25:33,742
I just opened the
drawer, and there it was.

405
00:25:33,766 --> 00:25:34,998
Some instincts, huh?

406
00:25:35,034 --> 00:25:36,700
How about that.

407
00:25:47,279 --> 00:25:50,314
Honest Earl's out
there looking at my car.

408
00:25:50,349 --> 00:25:51,960
I'm going to go talk to him.

409
00:25:51,984 --> 00:25:53,761
When I get his back to the door,

410
00:25:53,785 --> 00:25:55,419
you get out of here.

411
00:25:57,890 --> 00:25:59,222
Oh, Mr. Edwards.

412
00:25:59,258 --> 00:26:01,124
I've been looking
for you all over.

413
00:26:01,160 --> 00:26:02,370
Well, here I am... Honest Earl.

414
00:26:02,394 --> 00:26:03,471
Sorry if I kept you waiting,

415
00:26:03,495 --> 00:26:04,762
but I had to take these people

416
00:26:04,797 --> 00:26:06,797
for a test drive. Oh...
oh, that's okay, uh...

417
00:26:06,832 --> 00:26:08,732
Can't give you very
much on a trade-in.

418
00:26:08,768 --> 00:26:11,234
Uh... Why not?

419
00:26:11,269 --> 00:26:13,770
Well, this baby
needs a lot of work.

420
00:26:13,806 --> 00:26:15,338
Does not.

421
00:26:15,373 --> 00:26:17,018
This is the best
car I ever owned.

422
00:26:17,042 --> 00:26:18,075
Runs like a top.

423
00:26:18,110 --> 00:26:20,343
Not for very long.

424
00:26:20,379 --> 00:26:21,745
Ah, look over there.

425
00:26:21,781 --> 00:26:23,814
There's a car I could
put you in real cheap.

426
00:26:23,849 --> 00:26:25,448
Uh, Mr. Edwards...

427
00:26:25,484 --> 00:26:27,384
I'm not here to buy a car.

428
00:26:27,419 --> 00:26:29,152
I'm an attorney.

429
00:26:29,187 --> 00:26:30,387
Ben Matlock.

430
00:26:30,422 --> 00:26:32,056
I represent Les Calhoun,

431
00:26:32,091 --> 00:26:34,758
the man accused of
killing T.J. Cassidy.

432
00:26:37,463 --> 00:26:38,762
Uh, Mr. Edwards?

433
00:26:38,798 --> 00:26:40,108
What is it, Mr. Matlock?

434
00:26:40,132 --> 00:26:42,365
Where were you at
the time of the murder?

435
00:26:42,401 --> 00:26:43,645
I didn't kill him.

436
00:26:43,669 --> 00:26:45,836
No, I didn't... I
didn't say you did.

437
00:26:45,871 --> 00:26:48,016
I just asked where
you were at the time.

438
00:26:48,040 --> 00:26:50,373
I was here doing my paperwork.

439
00:26:50,409 --> 00:26:52,910
Uh, my client said
he drove by here

440
00:26:52,945 --> 00:26:54,144
on the way to Brenner's lot,

441
00:26:54,179 --> 00:26:56,013
and the light was
on all right, but he...

442
00:26:56,048 --> 00:26:57,681
he-he said he didn't see you.

443
00:26:57,717 --> 00:26:59,116
If you had been working here,

444
00:26:59,151 --> 00:27:00,584
he would have seen you.

445
00:27:00,620 --> 00:27:02,664
Maybe I was in the john.

446
00:27:02,688 --> 00:27:04,165
How the hell do I know?

447
00:27:04,189 --> 00:27:05,200
I was here.

448
00:27:05,224 --> 00:27:06,790
Anything else?

449
00:27:06,825 --> 00:27:09,059
Uh... anybody else you know of

450
00:27:09,095 --> 00:27:10,872
would have wanted
Mr. Cassidy dead?

451
00:27:10,896 --> 00:27:12,440
Sure.

452
00:27:12,464 --> 00:27:15,365
Anybody who ever
bought a car from that jerk.

453
00:27:15,400 --> 00:27:18,302
He was a disgrace
to our profession.

454
00:27:18,337 --> 00:27:20,315
Well, why did you want to
do business with the man?

455
00:27:20,339 --> 00:27:22,239
Who says I did?

456
00:27:22,274 --> 00:27:24,975
I saw the contract.
You saw what?

457
00:27:25,010 --> 00:27:27,144
You tried to get him
to leave Brenner's

458
00:27:27,179 --> 00:27:28,857
and come over here
to you, didn't you?

459
00:27:28,881 --> 00:27:30,247
Absolutely not.

460
00:27:30,282 --> 00:27:31,660
And he turned you
down, didn't he?

461
00:27:31,684 --> 00:27:33,584
Beat it.

462
00:27:35,988 --> 00:27:38,021
Don't have to get ugly about it.

463
00:27:46,498 --> 00:27:47,876
What are you doing here now?

464
00:27:47,900 --> 00:27:49,833
Well, I'd just like to
ask you a question.

465
00:27:49,868 --> 00:27:50,834
I'm busy.

466
00:27:50,869 --> 00:27:52,703
I wonder if you'd
let me take a look

467
00:27:52,738 --> 00:27:54,638
at the contract you
had with T.J. Cassidy.

468
00:27:54,673 --> 00:27:56,417
That contract is
none of your business.

469
00:27:56,441 --> 00:27:57,886
Now, get the hell out of here.

470
00:27:57,910 --> 00:27:59,509
Either, uh, you
let me look at it

471
00:27:59,544 --> 00:28:00,978
or I can subpoena it.

472
00:28:01,013 --> 00:28:03,046
Either way, I'm
confident I'll find

473
00:28:03,081 --> 00:28:04,548
that your contract with him

474
00:28:04,583 --> 00:28:07,183
was about to or
had already expired.

475
00:28:07,218 --> 00:28:10,120
Now, listen, I renewed T.J.'s
contract every two years.

476
00:28:10,155 --> 00:28:13,090
If it was about to expire, I
would have renewed it again.

477
00:28:13,125 --> 00:28:14,869
Unless he planned
to go someplace else.

478
00:28:14,893 --> 00:28:16,805
You were aware that
he was negotiating

479
00:28:16,829 --> 00:28:18,462
with Honest Earl Edwards.

480
00:28:18,497 --> 00:28:20,108
That's what you were
fighting with him about

481
00:28:20,132 --> 00:28:21,142
the day of the murder.

482
00:28:21,166 --> 00:28:22,244
Not his expense account.

483
00:28:22,268 --> 00:28:23,268
You had lost him.

484
00:28:23,302 --> 00:28:24,901
I'm aware of no such thing.

485
00:28:24,937 --> 00:28:26,203
Then, let me see his contract

486
00:28:26,238 --> 00:28:27,648
with his name
on the bottom line.

487
00:28:27,672 --> 00:28:29,639
Get the hell out of here!

488
00:28:43,788 --> 00:28:48,158
Now... I let you
come along on this.

489
00:28:48,193 --> 00:28:50,071
And nothing may happen.

490
00:28:50,095 --> 00:28:51,661
But something may.

491
00:28:51,696 --> 00:28:52,974
And if it does,

492
00:28:52,998 --> 00:28:55,076
you've got to do
what I say, okay?

493
00:28:55,100 --> 00:28:57,033
Okay.

494
00:29:01,006 --> 00:29:02,505
I don't have a pickle.

495
00:29:02,540 --> 00:29:04,774
If she's going out,
she better hurry.

496
00:29:04,810 --> 00:29:06,076
It's getting late.

497
00:29:07,680 --> 00:29:10,180
Everybody else has a pickle.

498
00:29:10,215 --> 00:29:12,783
And you have a hard-boiled egg.

499
00:29:14,887 --> 00:29:16,920
Well, how about this?

500
00:29:16,956 --> 00:29:18,688
I'll give you

501
00:29:18,724 --> 00:29:22,159
my hard-boiled egg and my pickle

502
00:29:22,194 --> 00:29:25,129
if you promise
to stay in the car.

503
00:29:25,164 --> 00:29:26,596
Promise?

504
00:29:26,632 --> 00:29:28,431
Okay.

505
00:29:28,467 --> 00:29:29,477
Uh-oh.

506
00:29:29,501 --> 00:29:30,781
Showtime.

507
00:30:30,495 --> 00:30:32,228
What's going on in there?

508
00:30:32,264 --> 00:30:34,564
Beats me.

509
00:30:34,599 --> 00:30:37,934
Now, listen... you
stay in the car.

510
00:30:37,970 --> 00:30:39,502
You promised.

511
00:30:45,577 --> 00:30:47,510
♪ ♪

512
00:32:07,125 --> 00:32:08,770
Well, that was
sure some sideline...

513
00:32:08,794 --> 00:32:10,204
You know, maybe T.J. found out

514
00:32:10,228 --> 00:32:11,906
what you were doing
and wanted to be cut in.

515
00:32:11,930 --> 00:32:14,363
Maybe you figured out a way
to permanently cut him out.

516
00:32:14,399 --> 00:32:15,865
Oh, wait a minute,
wait a minute.

517
00:32:15,901 --> 00:32:19,069
Maybe we were selling
hot cars off Brenner's lot.

518
00:32:19,104 --> 00:32:20,536
But we didn't kill T.J.

519
00:32:20,572 --> 00:32:22,416
And if you're asking
if we have an alibi

520
00:32:22,440 --> 00:32:24,640
for the night he
was killed, here it is.

521
00:32:26,712 --> 00:32:30,079
Well, I really thought
that chop shop

522
00:32:30,115 --> 00:32:32,460
was our answer, I really did.

523
00:32:32,484 --> 00:32:34,651
What is a chop shop?

524
00:32:34,686 --> 00:32:36,453
Well, you were there.

525
00:32:36,488 --> 00:32:38,455
Cars and parts everywhere.

526
00:32:38,490 --> 00:32:40,368
It's where they
take hot cars apart.

527
00:32:40,392 --> 00:32:43,627
Take stolen cars apart. Hmm...

528
00:32:43,662 --> 00:32:45,928
Well. Well... Doggone it.

529
00:32:47,298 --> 00:32:49,365
If what George
Brenner says is true,

530
00:32:49,401 --> 00:32:52,368
that he and Jessie were the
only two with access to the keys.

531
00:32:52,404 --> 00:32:54,704
And we know where Jessie was,

532
00:32:54,740 --> 00:32:56,907
then I guess it has
to have been George.

533
00:32:58,010 --> 00:32:59,308
Well, couldn't Honest Earl

534
00:32:59,344 --> 00:33:01,377
have stolen a key? Made a copy?

535
00:33:01,413 --> 00:33:03,146
I don't see hardly how he could.

536
00:33:03,181 --> 00:33:05,649
It was really locked up tight.

537
00:33:05,684 --> 00:33:07,617
I don't see how he
could've gotten in there.

538
00:33:07,652 --> 00:33:12,089
Hmm... Chop shop, huh?

539
00:33:12,124 --> 00:33:14,157
Say you need a...

540
00:33:14,192 --> 00:33:16,626
new radiator for your car.

541
00:33:18,130 --> 00:33:20,763
You go to a regular
mechanic, he installs one, right?

542
00:33:20,799 --> 00:33:22,699
That's what I always thought.

543
00:33:22,735 --> 00:33:26,503
Unless you go to a garage
that deals with a chop shop.

544
00:33:26,538 --> 00:33:30,072
Then they get a radiator
out of a stolen car...

545
00:33:30,108 --> 00:33:31,619
Same make and
model as your car...

546
00:33:31,643 --> 00:33:33,843
Put it in your car...

547
00:33:33,878 --> 00:33:35,077
It's nearly all profit.

548
00:33:35,113 --> 00:33:36,858
You want a water
pump, same thing.

549
00:33:36,882 --> 00:33:38,359
You never know the difference.

550
00:33:38,383 --> 00:33:40,316
Anything: gas cap...

551
00:33:47,059 --> 00:33:48,491
Well, I'll be...

552
00:33:57,569 --> 00:33:59,780
It's hard to believe, but,
uh, parts of these cars

553
00:33:59,804 --> 00:34:02,071
are probably in a
dozen other cars by now.

554
00:34:02,107 --> 00:34:03,973
Mm. Hmm.

555
00:34:04,008 --> 00:34:05,319
Get? Yep.

556
00:34:05,343 --> 00:34:07,277
Oh! Okay.

557
00:34:15,320 --> 00:34:17,520
Doesn't work.

558
00:34:18,723 --> 00:34:20,823
<i>I observed the decedent to be</i>

559
00:34:20,859 --> 00:34:23,660
<i>lying beneath the front
portion of the vehicle.</i>

560
00:34:23,695 --> 00:34:26,162
In addition to that,

561
00:34:26,197 --> 00:34:29,599
what else did you see
which appeared to suggest

562
00:34:29,635 --> 00:34:32,802
the victim was killed by
his very own automobile?

563
00:34:32,838 --> 00:34:34,670
I observed fresh blood,

564
00:34:34,706 --> 00:34:37,006
uh, skin tissue
and bone fragments

565
00:34:37,041 --> 00:34:39,576
actually embedded in
the front grill of the vehicle.

566
00:34:39,611 --> 00:34:43,480
According to the testimony
of the officers on the scene,

567
00:34:43,515 --> 00:34:45,549
they discovered the defendant

568
00:34:45,584 --> 00:34:47,917
behind the wheel of
the murder weapon.

569
00:34:47,953 --> 00:34:49,852
Was there any physical evidence

570
00:34:49,888 --> 00:34:51,821
linking Mr. Calhoun
to the crime?

571
00:34:51,856 --> 00:34:54,524
His fingerprints were
on the steering wheel.

572
00:34:54,559 --> 00:34:55,758
Thank you, Lieutenant.

573
00:34:55,794 --> 00:34:56,893
That will be all.

574
00:34:58,129 --> 00:34:59,229
Mr. Matlock.

575
00:34:59,264 --> 00:35:02,131
No questions, Your Honor.

576
00:35:02,167 --> 00:35:03,566
"No questions"?!

577
00:35:03,601 --> 00:35:05,913
Why aren't you going to
ask him any questions?

578
00:35:05,937 --> 00:35:07,614
The more he talks,
the worse it gets.

579
00:35:07,638 --> 00:35:08,771
Yeah, but...

580
00:35:10,508 --> 00:35:12,709
Uh, Defense calls

581
00:35:12,744 --> 00:35:14,911
Honest Earl
Edwards to the stand.

582
00:35:14,946 --> 00:35:20,150
Mr. Edwards, you own
Honest Earl's Used Car Lot

583
00:35:20,185 --> 00:35:22,318
on Jackson
Boulevard, that right?

584
00:35:22,353 --> 00:35:23,787
Yes, sir, I do.

585
00:35:23,822 --> 00:35:25,688
That's quite an accomplishment.

586
00:35:25,723 --> 00:35:29,825
You were a mechanic and
now you own your own lot.

587
00:35:29,861 --> 00:35:31,528
Yeah, it's the American dream.

588
00:35:31,563 --> 00:35:33,029
Yeah, yeah.

589
00:35:33,065 --> 00:35:35,665
And then three years ago,

590
00:35:35,701 --> 00:35:38,568
George Brenner moved
in across the street,

591
00:35:38,604 --> 00:35:40,203
but you still did okay.

592
00:35:40,238 --> 00:35:41,771
I like to think so.

593
00:35:41,806 --> 00:35:43,039
Yeah.

594
00:35:43,074 --> 00:35:45,241
Then two years ago,

595
00:35:45,276 --> 00:35:50,179
George Brenner joined
up with T.J. Cassidy.

596
00:35:50,214 --> 00:35:53,683
And the more of those
crazy commercials he did,

597
00:35:53,718 --> 00:35:56,185
the more business
George Brenner did,

598
00:35:56,220 --> 00:35:58,888
and the more you had
to struggle to survive.

599
00:35:58,924 --> 00:36:01,057
Isn't that right? I do okay.

600
00:36:04,829 --> 00:36:07,797
You tried to get T.J. Cassidy

601
00:36:07,832 --> 00:36:09,643
to leave Brenner's
and come to your lot.

602
00:36:09,667 --> 00:36:11,145
But he refused.
Isn't that right?

603
00:36:11,169 --> 00:36:14,303
Hey, I'd rather the guy work
for me than the competition.

604
00:36:14,339 --> 00:36:16,305
But it was no big deal.

605
00:36:16,341 --> 00:36:18,108
No? Why not?

606
00:36:18,143 --> 00:36:20,543
Because T.J.
brought in customers.

607
00:36:20,578 --> 00:36:22,824
And if they didn't find the
car they wanted at Brenner's,

608
00:36:22,848 --> 00:36:24,959
they'd come right across
the street to my place,

609
00:36:24,983 --> 00:36:26,716
so everybody benefited.

610
00:36:26,751 --> 00:36:28,685
Yeah.

611
00:36:30,522 --> 00:36:33,589
You really needed all the
help you could get, didn't you?

612
00:36:33,625 --> 00:36:35,458
Told you.

613
00:36:35,493 --> 00:36:36,826
I'm doing fine.

614
00:36:36,862 --> 00:36:39,996
Mr. Edwards,

615
00:36:40,031 --> 00:36:42,832
you're in deep
financial trouble.

616
00:36:45,370 --> 00:36:47,570
I've had a couple
of slow months.

617
00:36:47,605 --> 00:36:49,339
According to the bank,

618
00:36:49,374 --> 00:36:51,474
you've had a
couple of slow years.

619
00:36:51,510 --> 00:36:54,911
And if things don't improve,

620
00:36:54,946 --> 00:36:56,824
you're going to have to go
into bankruptcy, aren't you?

621
00:36:56,848 --> 00:36:57,959
Objection!

622
00:36:57,983 --> 00:36:59,927
Argumentative. No
relevancy, Your Honor.

623
00:36:59,951 --> 00:37:02,151
Sustained.

624
00:37:02,186 --> 00:37:04,621
I'll tell you what I think.

625
00:37:04,656 --> 00:37:06,890
I think you knew you
had to do something.

626
00:37:06,925 --> 00:37:09,693
Your business was
going downhill, and fast.

627
00:37:11,362 --> 00:37:13,229
Well, what you decided,

628
00:37:13,264 --> 00:37:16,199
instead of getting
rid of your debts,

629
00:37:16,234 --> 00:37:19,135
you would get rid
of your competition.

630
00:37:19,170 --> 00:37:20,503
No, that's crazy.

631
00:37:20,538 --> 00:37:23,907
That meant getting rid
of T.J. Cassidy, didn't it?

632
00:37:23,942 --> 00:37:25,508
Objection!

633
00:37:25,544 --> 00:37:27,488
No foundation. It's
argumentative, Your Honor.

634
00:37:27,512 --> 00:37:29,257
If the court will bear with me,

635
00:37:29,281 --> 00:37:31,392
Your Honor. Please come
to the point, Mr. Matlock.

636
00:37:31,416 --> 00:37:32,916
Yes, sir.

637
00:37:32,951 --> 00:37:35,151
I believe

638
00:37:35,187 --> 00:37:39,555
that you not only wanted
to get rid of T.J. Cassidy,

639
00:37:39,590 --> 00:37:43,392
you wanted to destroy
George Brenner's business.

640
00:37:43,428 --> 00:37:45,561
And what better way to do that,

641
00:37:45,596 --> 00:37:49,432
than to have T.J. run
over by his own car.

642
00:37:49,467 --> 00:37:53,135
The one he used in all
those TV commercials.

643
00:37:56,375 --> 00:37:59,476
You claim the
night of the murder...

644
00:38:01,146 --> 00:38:03,413
you were in your office working.

645
00:38:03,448 --> 00:38:05,414
Yes.

646
00:38:05,450 --> 00:38:06,493
You were alone?

647
00:38:06,517 --> 00:38:07,561
No one came and no one called?

648
00:38:07,585 --> 00:38:09,118
That's right.

649
00:38:09,153 --> 00:38:12,121
So you can't prove
that's where you were?

650
00:38:13,791 --> 00:38:17,627
You don't have what we call
a "substantiated alibi," do you?

651
00:38:17,662 --> 00:38:19,862
No, I guess not.

652
00:38:21,833 --> 00:38:25,301
Uh, at this time, Your Honor,

653
00:38:25,337 --> 00:38:29,539
with the court's permission,
uh, I'd like to move

654
00:38:29,574 --> 00:38:31,614
to the matter we
discussed in chambers.

655
00:38:31,643 --> 00:38:34,243
People restate their objections.

656
00:38:34,278 --> 00:38:35,812
So noted.

657
00:38:38,683 --> 00:38:41,317
<i>Now, Mr. Edwards,</i>

658
00:38:41,352 --> 00:38:44,988
<i>uh, you have testified
that you are a mechanic.</i>

659
00:38:46,190 --> 00:38:49,358
Now, in your expert opinion,

660
00:38:49,394 --> 00:38:52,428
would you tell me why it is

661
00:38:52,463 --> 00:38:56,365
when I bring my
car in for repairs,

662
00:38:56,401 --> 00:38:58,300
it costs so much money?

663
00:38:58,336 --> 00:39:00,169
Labor's expensive.

664
00:39:00,204 --> 00:39:01,916
Yeah. Yeah, yeah.

665
00:39:01,940 --> 00:39:03,150
And parts.

666
00:39:03,174 --> 00:39:05,040
Oh, yes. Parts have gone way up.

667
00:39:05,076 --> 00:39:08,211
So, when you buy
used cars for your lot,

668
00:39:08,246 --> 00:39:09,824
you must do a lot
of work on them.

669
00:39:09,848 --> 00:39:11,080
Yes, we do.

670
00:39:11,115 --> 00:39:14,517
So, I guess you have
a lot of parts around.

671
00:39:14,552 --> 00:39:15,918
Yes.

672
00:39:15,953 --> 00:39:17,899
Do you sometimes
do the work yourself?

673
00:39:17,923 --> 00:39:19,222
Yes.

674
00:39:19,257 --> 00:39:20,857
Uh-huh.

675
00:39:20,892 --> 00:39:23,793
Now, as you see here
with the court's permission,

676
00:39:23,828 --> 00:39:26,962
I have had, uh, the
murder weapon...

677
00:39:26,998 --> 00:39:30,833
Uh, T.J.'s car... Brought
here to Brenner's lot.

678
00:39:30,868 --> 00:39:35,471
And, uh... This here...

679
00:39:35,507 --> 00:39:40,310
is, uh, People's Exhibit Seven.

680
00:39:42,413 --> 00:39:46,749
This is the key that was
found in T.J.'s pocket

681
00:39:46,785 --> 00:39:48,484
at the time of the murder.

682
00:39:49,654 --> 00:39:51,465
Now, would you just
walk over here with me?

683
00:39:51,489 --> 00:39:52,455
Sure. Just right over here.

684
00:39:52,490 --> 00:39:53,901
Right over here to the car.

685
00:39:53,925 --> 00:39:56,659
Okay, now, I'll open her up.

686
00:39:56,694 --> 00:39:58,706
And you just get right in.

687
00:39:58,730 --> 00:40:00,229
It's okay. Go ahead.

688
00:40:00,264 --> 00:40:01,409
There you go.

689
00:40:01,433 --> 00:40:04,400
Now, take the
key, start the car.

690
00:40:09,107 --> 00:40:10,506
Doesn't fit, does it?

691
00:40:10,542 --> 00:40:13,343
No. Wonder why?

692
00:40:13,378 --> 00:40:14,677
Wrong key.

693
00:40:14,713 --> 00:40:16,846
"Wrong key."

694
00:40:16,881 --> 00:40:18,815
I don't think so.
Come over here.

695
00:40:20,218 --> 00:40:21,818
Show you something.

696
00:40:23,221 --> 00:40:29,959
This... is Defense Exhibit "H."

697
00:40:31,062 --> 00:40:32,973
This was T.J.'s spare key,

698
00:40:32,997 --> 00:40:34,930
found in his home.

699
00:40:34,966 --> 00:40:38,033
It's identical to the key

700
00:40:38,069 --> 00:40:40,548
that was found in his pocket.

701
00:40:40,572 --> 00:40:45,375
These are the
keys to T.J.'s car.

702
00:40:45,410 --> 00:40:48,043
Why won't they start it?

703
00:40:48,079 --> 00:40:50,012
I don't know.

704
00:40:52,584 --> 00:40:56,385
Well... you're a mechanic.

705
00:40:56,420 --> 00:41:00,890
Um... what, uh, what about this.

706
00:41:00,925 --> 00:41:05,962
What... what if that's
not the original ignition

707
00:41:05,997 --> 00:41:07,396
that was in T.J.'s car.

708
00:41:07,431 --> 00:41:09,410
What about that?
What do you mean?

709
00:41:09,434 --> 00:41:11,167
Uh, ignitions can be

710
00:41:11,202 --> 00:41:13,236
interchanged
sometimes, can't they?

711
00:41:13,271 --> 00:41:15,638
I mean, adapted. Yes.

712
00:41:15,673 --> 00:41:19,742
Well, that would
explain why T.J.'s keys

713
00:41:19,777 --> 00:41:22,177
won't start his own car.

714
00:41:22,213 --> 00:41:23,679
Wouldn't it?

715
00:41:23,715 --> 00:41:25,047
I guess it could.

716
00:41:25,083 --> 00:41:29,185
So if that were
true, then someone

717
00:41:29,220 --> 00:41:31,521
must've snuck into Brenner's lot

718
00:41:31,556 --> 00:41:34,023
the night of the murder,

719
00:41:34,059 --> 00:41:37,193
popped the ignition
out of T.J.'s car,

720
00:41:37,228 --> 00:41:41,297
replaced that ignition
with another one.

721
00:41:41,332 --> 00:41:43,699
So that that person

722
00:41:43,734 --> 00:41:48,304
could get in T.J.'s
car, start it up,

723
00:41:48,339 --> 00:41:50,239
and run him over.

724
00:41:50,275 --> 00:41:52,208
That's possible, isn't it?

725
00:41:52,243 --> 00:41:53,923
I don't know.

726
00:41:55,413 --> 00:41:58,881
But if we can find an ignition

727
00:41:58,916 --> 00:42:02,885
that the key that was
found in T.J.'s pocket

728
00:42:02,920 --> 00:42:05,399
would turn over, then we'd know

729
00:42:05,423 --> 00:42:07,368
that was the original ignition

730
00:42:07,392 --> 00:42:09,770
that was in T.J.'s
car, wouldn't we?

731
00:42:09,794 --> 00:42:12,005
And we would have
a pretty good idea

732
00:42:12,029 --> 00:42:14,763
who the murderer
was, wouldn't we?

733
00:42:14,799 --> 00:42:16,165
I don't know.

734
00:42:18,269 --> 00:42:21,103
You think these keys

735
00:42:21,138 --> 00:42:23,405
would fit any of
the cars on your lot?

736
00:42:23,441 --> 00:42:24,874
No.

737
00:42:24,910 --> 00:42:26,976
Want to try a few
just for fun, you know?

738
00:42:27,012 --> 00:42:28,956
No. Your Honor,

739
00:42:28,980 --> 00:42:31,347
we will be here all afternoon.

740
00:42:31,382 --> 00:42:33,182
I don't think so, Your Honor.

741
00:42:34,319 --> 00:42:38,354
Uh, Mr. Edwards,

742
00:42:38,389 --> 00:42:41,357
you recognize this
little, black truck?

743
00:42:41,392 --> 00:42:44,293
No, I don't.

744
00:42:44,329 --> 00:42:49,264
Mr. Edwards, this
little, black truck

745
00:42:49,300 --> 00:42:51,266
is from your lot.

746
00:42:55,239 --> 00:42:57,573
Would you please
just step over here

747
00:42:57,608 --> 00:43:00,109
and get in this truck?

748
00:43:00,144 --> 00:43:02,978
No.

749
00:43:03,014 --> 00:43:04,147
Please?

750
00:43:04,182 --> 00:43:05,515
No.

751
00:43:05,550 --> 00:43:08,484
Come on.

752
00:43:08,519 --> 00:43:10,919
Your Honor, would
you instruct the witness

753
00:43:10,955 --> 00:43:12,155
to do as he's told?

754
00:43:12,190 --> 00:43:14,290
Mr. Edwards.

755
00:43:25,703 --> 00:43:26,947
There you are.

756
00:43:26,971 --> 00:43:29,531
Now, here you
go. Go right on in.

757
00:43:30,141 --> 00:43:32,007
There you are.

758
00:43:32,043 --> 00:43:33,809
There you go.

759
00:43:35,046 --> 00:43:37,313
There's the key.

760
00:43:39,016 --> 00:43:40,115
Start it.

761
00:43:52,497 --> 00:43:54,457
You changed the
ignition, didn't you?

762
00:43:56,567 --> 00:43:59,535
But you have the
mind of a mechanic,

763
00:43:59,571 --> 00:44:01,937
and you couldn't
see throwing away

764
00:44:01,973 --> 00:44:04,873
a perfectly good ignition.

765
00:44:04,909 --> 00:44:06,909
So you put it in this car.

766
00:44:09,113 --> 00:44:11,447
You ran him over, didn't you?

767
00:44:13,684 --> 00:44:15,362
You don't have to tell me.

768
00:44:15,386 --> 00:44:17,987
Maybe I'll read
about it in the paper.

769
00:44:20,191 --> 00:44:22,591
Nothing further.

770
00:44:22,626 --> 00:44:24,426
Did you hear that?!

771
00:44:24,461 --> 00:44:26,862
He ran him over! He
changed the ignition.

772
00:44:26,897 --> 00:44:28,263
Did you just hear that?!

773
00:44:28,299 --> 00:44:29,442
Les, Les, Les...

774
00:44:29,466 --> 00:44:31,233
That was great!
It was just great!

775
00:44:31,268 --> 00:44:33,346
You can't, you can't, you
can't talk to the jury, Les.

776
00:44:33,370 --> 00:44:34,381
You can't, Ace. I...

777
00:44:34,405 --> 00:44:36,965
You can't talk to the jury.

778
00:44:43,381 --> 00:44:46,949
Well, that was some
experience. Yeah.

779
00:44:50,221 --> 00:44:52,955
Try to hang a murder
one on me, huh?

780
00:44:52,990 --> 00:44:54,534
You know, you
really had that guy.

781
00:44:54,558 --> 00:44:55,702
You really had him.

782
00:44:55,726 --> 00:44:57,826
Well, I felt like I was
on the right track.

783
00:44:57,862 --> 00:45:00,829
You did great.
Well... Just great!

784
00:45:00,865 --> 00:45:01,964
Mm-hmm.

785
00:45:04,168 --> 00:45:05,801
Real nice neighborhood.

786
00:45:05,837 --> 00:45:08,070
Yeah. You lived here long?

787
00:45:08,105 --> 00:45:09,505
Since the war.

788
00:45:09,540 --> 00:45:11,640
Long time. Yeah.

789
00:45:13,010 --> 00:45:14,910
You ever notice
when guys our age

790
00:45:14,945 --> 00:45:17,212
talk about the war,
nobody asks which one?

791
00:45:17,248 --> 00:45:19,288
Yeah.

792
00:45:21,185 --> 00:45:22,952
We've seen it all, huh?

793
00:45:22,987 --> 00:45:25,154
Oh, yeah. Yeah.

794
00:45:25,189 --> 00:45:27,134
We're a lot alike, you and me.

795
00:45:27,158 --> 00:45:29,826
I suppose so. Yeah.

796
00:45:29,861 --> 00:45:31,660
